/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A batch drying test uses a thin sample in air at fixed dry-bulb temperature, humidity and velocity.
   - R is drying rate in kg/(m²·h); ms is dry-solid mass in kg; A is drying area in m²; X is moisture in kg/kg dry solid; t is time in h.
   - In the constant-rate period the surface is wet.
   - The constant rate can be predicted from the air.
   - Rc is the constant drying rate in kg/(m²·s); h is the heat-transfer coefficient in W/(m²·K); T and Tw are the dry-bulb and wet-bulb temperatures of the air; λw is the latent heat at Tw in J/kg; G is the air mass velocity in kg/(m²·h).
   - tc is constant-rate time in h; X₁ is initial moisture; Xc is critical moisture; Rc is constant drying rate; other symbols as before.
   - tf is falling-rate time in h; Xc − X* is free moisture at the critical point; Rc is the constant drying rate in kg/(m²·h); A is drying area in m²; ms is dry-solid mass in kg; X₂ is final moisture above X*; ln is the natural logarithm.
   - If X₂ is above Xc, only tc applies.
   - Higher air temperature and lower humidity raise Rc and often Xc, because evaporation is faster.
*/
