/* book Worked example 2: Q: Air at 65 °C with Y = 0.010 kg/kg dry air flows at 3.5 m/s parallel to the surface of a tray of wet solid. Its wet-bulb temperature is 28.8 °C and its humid volume 0.973 m³/kg dry air. Predict the constant drying rate.
   1. Air density: (1 + 0.010)/0.973 = 1.038 kg/m³.
   2. Mass velocity: G = 1.038 × 3.5 × 3600 = 13 080 kg/(m²·h), inside the range of the correlation.
   3. Heat-transfer coefficient: h = 0.0204 × 13 0800.8 = 0.0204 × 1964 = 40.1 W/(m²·K).
   4. Latent heat at 28.8 °C: λw ≈ 2433 kJ/kg = 2.433 × 10⁶ J/kg.
   5. Rc = 40.1 × (65 − 28.8)/(2.433 × 10⁶) = 5.97 × 10⁻⁴ kg/(m²·s) = 2.15 kg/(m²·h).
   Answer: The constant drying rate is about 2.15 kg/(m²·h). Doubling the air speed would raise it by 20.8 = 1.74 times. */
