/* book: lead: Dryers remove water from a wet solid by bringing it into contact with hot, dry air. Batch dryers dry a fixed lot; continuous dryers take a steady feed and give a steady product. The choice follows the solid: slabs, pastes, slurries and granules each need a different contact pattern. In through-circulation drying, air is forced through a bed of granular solid, so the air picks up water and cools as */
/* book Figure 7.8.1: The worked example. Air enters the bed at 60 °C with Y = 0.010 and, while the solid is wet, cools along its adiabatic line as it climbs. Picking up 0.012 kg/kg it leaves at 31.5 °C and about 75 % RH, short of its limit, the adiabatic saturation point 27.6 °C with Y = 0.0236; that gap is ln 8.3 = 2.1 */
