/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Start with the bone-dry solid S, kg/h.
   - W is water evaporated, kg/h; S is bone-dry solid, kg/h; F and P are wet feed and product, kg/h; x₁ and x₂ are wet-basis mass fractions; X₁ and X₂ are dry-basis kg water per kg dry solid.
   - The dry air carries that water: mₐ = W/(Y₂ − Y₁), where mₐ is kg dry air/h and Y is kg water per kg dry air.
   - Useful heat has three parts: evaporate water, heat the solids, heat the water left in the product.
   - Quseful is useful heat, kJ/h; λ is latent heat at the wet-bulb temperature, about 2400 kJ/kg; cₚₛ is specific heat of dry solid, kJ/(kg·K); cₚw is 4.18 kJ/(kg·K); T₁ and T₂ are solid inlet and outlet temperatures, °C.
   - A rotary dryer is a slightly inclined cylinder, 0.5° to 5° to the horizontal, turning at 1 to 8 rpm.
   - Fan volumetric flow uses humid volume at the outlet: vH = (1/29 + Y/18) × 22.4 × (T + 273)/273 m³/kg dry air.
   - Inside the shell are longitudinal fins called flights.
   - In cocurrent, air and solids move the same way.
   - Use a rotary dryer for free-flowing granular solids, not for pastes that ball up.
*/
