/* book Worked example 2: Q: For the same dryer, the solids enter at 25 °C and leave at 70 °C. The dry solid has cₚₛ = 1.2 kJ/(kg·K). Find the useful heat duty (kJ/h) and the fraction used for evaporation.
   1. Heat to evaporate: Q₁ = 234.7 × 2400 = 563 280 kJ/h.
   2. Heat to raise dry solid: Q₂ = 750 × 1.2 × (70 − 25) = 40 500 kJ/h.
   3. Heat to raise remaining water: Q₃ = 15.3 × 4.18 × 45 = 2878 kJ/h.
   4. Useful duty: Quseful = 563 280 + 40 500 + 2878 = 606 658 kJ/h.
   5. Fraction of useful heat used for evaporation = Q₁/Quseful = 0.929, that is 92.9 %. This is not the dryer efficiency.
   6. Drying efficiency = W·λ/Qfuel. Get Qfuel from the air-side balance; it is typically 40 to 70 %.
   Answer: Useful heat duty about 606 700 kJ/h; 92.9 % of it goes to evaporation. Dryer efficiency needs the fuel duty from the air side. */
