/* book exam traps: Using wet-basis percentage as kg water per kg dry solid. Convert before the balance. | Using total humid air mass instead of dry air mass in the volumetric flow, so the fan is oversized. | Forgetting sensible heat for solids and remaining moisture; the duty comes out too low and the air heater is undersized. | Taking fan volumetric flow at inlet temperature. Air cools and gains water; the outlet humid volume is usually smaller than the hot inlet volume, so using inlet T oversizes the fan. | Mixing up cocurrent and counter-current: cocurrent protects heat-sensitive solids but its exhaust gas leaves warmer, so thermal efficiency is lower. */
